            %p
              The second fact gives the minus sign under the brace, which makes the total expression vanish, if the Dirac field and jet coordinates indeed are anti-commuting (which, incidentally, means that we found an “
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/off-shell"} off-shell
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conserved+current"} conserved current
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DiracCurrent"}
              below).
            %p
              If however the Dirac field and jet coordinates did commute with each other, we would instead have a plus sign under the brace, in which case the total horizontal derivative expression above would equal the massless Dirac field Lagrangian
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:DiracFieldLagrangianMassless"} (7)
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TrivialLagrangianDensities"}
            %p
              The same
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometric
              nature of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+field"} Dirac field
              will be necessary for its intended
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
              %em
