              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
              which is not a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
              is called an
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
          #FreeFieldTheoryRelevance.num_remark
            %h6#remark_6 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (relevance of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
              one considers
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theories"} field theories
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#InfinitesimalNeighbourhood"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+formal+smooth+set"} super smooth set
              of general
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
              are typically of limited interest in themselves, this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbation theory
              around them exhausts much of what is known about
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              in general, and therefore
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
              are of paramount importance for the general theory.
            %p
              We discuss the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
              below in
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Propagators"} Propagators
              and their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
              below in
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
          #EquationOfMotionOfFreeRealScalarField.num_prop
            %h6#example_16 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+scalar+field"} real scalar field
                is
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+scalar+field"} real scalar field
              from example
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LagrangianForFreeScalarFieldOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            %p
              By example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeScalarFieldEOM"}
              its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+form"} Euler-Lagrange form
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi EL
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi L
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ,
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %mo −
                      %msup
                        %mi m
                        %mn 2
                      %mo )
                    %mi δ
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi dvol
                      %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\delta_{EL}\mathbf{L}
                    \\;=\;
                    \\left(\eta^{\mu \nu} \phi_{,\mu \nu} - m^2 \right) \delta \phi \wedge dvol_\sigma
            %p
              Hence for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi \in \Gamma_\Sigma(E) = C^\infty(X)
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
              according to def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#EulerLagrangeEquationsOnTrivialVectorFieldBundleOverMinkowskiSpacetime"}
              is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi η
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mn 2
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                        %mo ∂
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi ν
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mi Φ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\eta^{\mu \nu} \frac{\partial^2 }{\partial x^\mu \partial x^\nu} \Phi - m^2 \Phi \;=\; 0
            %p often abbreviated as
            #eq:KleinGordonEquation.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (29)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Φ
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Box - m^2) \Phi \;=\; 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              This
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PDE"} PDE
              is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
              on Minowski spacetime. If the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
              vanishes,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m = 0
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  relativistic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+equation"} wave equation
            %p
              Hence this is indeed a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
              according to def.
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeFieldTheory"}
            %p
              The corresponding
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+differential+operator"} linear differential operator
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentialOperator"}
            #eq:KleinGordonOperator.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (30)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msub
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Σ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Box - m^2)
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\Gamma_\Sigma(\Sigma \times \mathbb{R})
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\Gamma_\Sigma(\Sigma \times \mathbb{R})
            %p
              is called the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
          %p
            For later use we record the following basic fact about the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
          #FormallySelfAdjointKleinGordonOperator.num_example
            %h6#example_17 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formally+adjoint+differential+operator"} formally self-adjoint
                )
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:KleinGordonOperator"} (30)
              is its own
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+adjoint+differential+operator"} formal adjoint
              (def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FormallyAdjointDifferentialOperators"}
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FormallyAdjointDifferentialOperatorWitness"} (?)
              given by
